---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="miles-to-km">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="miles-to-km" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The exact factor, and why it is exact</h2>
    <p>
      A statute mile is 5,280 feet. A foot is exactly 0.3048 m. Multiply and you get
      <strong>1,609.344 m</strong>, or 1.609344 km, exact to the last digit. It is two definitions
      multiplied together, with no measurement. Road-sign conversions round this number. This miles
      to km converter uses it exactly.
    </p>

    <h2>Running distances</h2>
    <p>
      Race distances are defined in metres and then described in miles, so the mile figures look
      untidy:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Race</th><th scope="col">Official distance</th><th scope="col">In miles</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5K</td><td>5,000 m</td><td>3.10686 mi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10K</td><td>10,000 m</td><td>6.21371 mi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Half marathon</td><td>21,097.5 m</td><td>13.10938 mi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marathon</td><td>42,195 m</td><td>26.21876 mi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mile</td><td>1,609.344 m</td><td>1 mi</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The marathon is not "26.2 miles rounded to 42.195 km". It is the other way round: the distance
      was fixed in metres in 1921, and 26.2 is the rounded description.
    </p>

    <h2>Speeds convert with the same number</h2>
    <p>
      Miles per hour to kilometres per hour uses the identical factor, because the hours cancel:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>30 mph = 48.28 km/h (a UK urban limit against a European 50)</li>
      <li>50 mph = 80.47 km/h</li>
      <li>60 mph = 96.56 km/h</li>
      <li>70 mph = 112.65 km/h (UK motorway against a French 130)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Speedometers can legally show too high a speed, never too low, usually by 1&ndash;3% plus a
      fixed offset. The conversion here is exact. Your dashboard shows a little more than your real
      speed on purpose.
    </p>

    <h2>Pace inverts</h2>
    <p>
      A pace is time per distance, so it converts in the opposite direction to a distance. To turn
      minutes per mile into minutes per kilometre, divide by 1.609344. An 8:00/mile pace is 4:58/km,
      and a 5:00/km pace is 8:03/mile. This is the most common conversion error in running, and the
      wrong direction makes you look about 60% faster.
    </p>

    <h2>Nautical miles are a different unit</h2>
    <p>
      A nautical mile is exactly 1,852 m (originally one minute of latitude), about 15% longer than a
      land mile. Aviation and marine distances, and the knot (one nautical mile per hour), use it. If
      your figure came from a chart or a flight plan, convert with 1.852, not 1.609344.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
